  that engage accessibility as an artistic material.\n\nMade in collaborati
 on with dancer Jerron Herman and poet and artist Joselia Rebekah Hughes\, 
 the central work\, Leans\, Reverses (2022)\, emerges from the relationship
  between a dance performance\, its recording\, and transcription (a choreo
 graphic score\, captions\, and audio description). The installation includ
 es t\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-09-04 09:46 PDT
LOCATION:Cantor Arts Center
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Cantor Arts] JANE! An intimate exploration of Gilded Age excess 
  and the enigmatic life and death of Jane Stanford
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260905
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260905
DTSTAMP:20260904T164637Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-52880326677126-52880513749794@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Curated by Associate Curator of European Art\, Patrick R. Crow
 ley\, JANE! celebrates the aesthetic and affective excesses of Jane Stanfo
 rd\, the Gilded Age founder and matriarch of Stanford University. Bringing
  together precious keepsakes\, souvenirs\, archaeological detritus\, and s
 pooky messages from beyond the grave\, the exhibition reveals the campy un
 derside of a familiar biography and perhaps an even more familiar murder m
 ystery.\n\nFrom her elegant French gowns to a colossal painting of her jew
 els\n\nAges: All ages\nSource: https://museum.stanford.edu/exhibitions/jan
 e\nLast synced: 2026-09-04 09:46 PDT
LOCATION:Cantor Arts Center
URL:https://museum.stanford.edu/exhibitions/jane
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[East Asia Library / Stanford University Libraries] Moments in the
  History of East Asian Printing
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260905
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260905
DTSTAMP:20260904T164637Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-53020733796497-53020733950197@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Moments in the History of East Asian Printing brings together 
 rare books\, facsimiles\, woodblocks\, broadsheets\, illustrated books\, t
 ypewriter materials\, and other objects from the East Asia Library’s col
 lections. The exhibit explores selected episodes in the history of printin
 g across China\, Korea\, and Japan\, from Buddhist woodblock printing and 
 movable type to lithography\, popular publishing\, travel prints\, visual 
 encyclopedias\, and the Chinese typewriter. Rather than offering a complet
 e chronol\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-09-04 09:46 PDT
LOCATION:Lathrop Library
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Stanford University Libraries] Text Machines: Scarlet Thread of t
 he Digital Order (1883-2026)
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260905
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260905
DTSTAMP:20260904T164637Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-53251159287291-53251159377500@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Stanford Libraries now holds an 1883 edition of a rare book\,
  Les laboureurs: poème tiré de Jocelyn\, whose pages (text and ornamen
 ts) were entirely woven on a Jacquard loom by the firm of silk merchant Jo
 seph-Alphonse Henry. This exhibition explores the cultural contexts and ma
 terial conditions of Les laboureurs’ production\, and considers parall
 els between technology-driven global change in the late nineteenth century
  and in our current day. The exhibition includes microscope images reveali
 ng the\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-09-04 09:46 PDT
LOCATION:Green Library\, Bing Wing
END:VEVENT
